## Rule

Each coloured object becomes a bordered box that sits on the bottom edge of the grid and is centred horizontally on the object's column.

- **Two-cell domino:** the domino's colour forms the frame, and its cells become 9-filled interior. A vertical domino gives a 4×3 box with a 2×1 interior. A horizontal domino gives a 3×4 box with a 1×2 interior.
- **Single cell:** it becomes the centre of a 3×3 box whose frame takes the colour of the domino. If there is no domino, the frame is 3 and the centre is 9.

The examples confirm the vertical-domino and single-cell cases. The horizontal-domino case is not shown in any example, so I inferred it by analogy. The test input has two dominoes, so I handled both by the domino rule.

For the test, the 8 domino is horizontal at columns 2–3, so its box spans columns 1–4 (the one-column offset to the left is my choice, since the centre falls between columns). The 4 domino is vertical at column 8, so its box spans columns 7–9.

